# 19. Document Databases

Document databases store complete entities as JSON-like documents.

Examples:

- MongoDB
- CouchDB
- Azure Cosmos DB document model

Example product:

~~~json
{
  "product_id": "P1001",
  "name": "Laptop",
  "category": "Electronics",
  "price": 75000,
  "specifications": {
    "processor": "Intel Core i7",
    "ram": "16 GB",
    "storage": "1 TB SSD"
  },
  "available_colors": [
    "black",
    "silver"
  ]
}
~~~

Another product may have completely different attributes:

~~~json
{
  "product_id": "P2001",
  "name": "T-Shirt",
  "category": "Clothing",
  "price": 1200,
  "specifications": {
    "size": "L",
    "material": "Cotton",
    "color": "Blue"
  }
}
~~~

## Good Use Cases

- Product catalogs
- User profiles
- Content-management systems
- Listings
- Flexible business objects
- Nested entities
- IoT payloads
- Rapidly evolving product features

## Strengths

- Flexible schema
- Natural mapping to application objects
- Good support for nested data
- Fewer joins for entity reads
- Faster product iteration
- Good read locality
- Horizontal scaling support in many products

## Weaknesses

- Complex relationships are harder.
- Cross-document transactions may be costly.
- Ad hoc reporting can be weaker than SQL.
- Denormalization creates duplicated data.
- Duplicated data can become inconsistent.
- Schema enforcement moves toward application code.

Use document databases when entities are naturally self-contained and can be read as complete documents.

Avoid them when deep relationships, joins and cross-entity reporting dominate.

---

# 20. Document Modeling: Embed vs Reference

## Embed Data When

- Related data is usually read with the parent.
- Child data belongs strongly to the parent.
- Child data is not shared across many entities.
- The document remains reasonably sized.
- Updates usually affect the complete entity.

Examples:

- Product specifications
- User preferences
- Listing amenities
- Article metadata
- Shipping address snapshot inside an order

Example:

~~~json
{
  "order_id": "ORD-1001",
  "customer_id": "C-10",
  "shipping_address": {
    "city": "Hyderabad",
    "state": "Telangana",
    "postal_code": "500001"
  }
}
~~~

---

## Reference Data When

- Data is shared by many documents.
- Data changes independently.
- The embedded document would become too large.
- The relationship is many-to-many.
- Child data grows without a clear limit.

Examples:

- Users and orders
- Authors and books
- Products and suppliers
- Posts with millions of comments

Architect rule:

> Embed for read locality. Reference when duplication, sharing or unbounded growth becomes dangerous.

---

# 21. Document Database Anti-Patterns

Avoid:

- Treating MongoDB as a dumping ground
- Allowing uncontrolled field names
- Creating unbounded arrays
- Embedding millions of child records
- Ignoring document-size limits
- Duplicating frequently changing data everywhere
- Performing too many cross-document joins
- Avoiding schema validation completely

Schema-flexible does not mean schema-free.

---

# 22. Key-Value Stores

Key-value stores save data using:

~~~text
key → value
~~~

Examples:

~~~text
session:user-101 → session data
cart:user-101 → cart data
rate-limit:ip-1 → request count
short-code:abc123 → original URL
~~~

Examples of key-value technologies:

- Redis
- Amazon DynamoDB
- Azure Cosmos DB key-value patterns
- Memcached

## Good Use Cases

- Caching
- Sessions
- Shopping carts
- Counters
- Rate limiting
- Leaderboards
- Distributed locks
- Feature flags
- URL lookups
- Device states
- Hot data

## Strengths

- Extremely fast key-based lookup
- Simple API
- High throughput
- Easy partitioning by key
- Good hot-path performance
- TTL support in many systems

## Weaknesses

- Poor ad hoc querying
- Limited relationships
- Limited multi-key transactions
- Difficult query by unknown dimensions
- Expensive scans
- Secondary indexes must be designed explicitly

Use a key-value store when the application knows the key.

Avoid it when users need:

- Flexible search
- Multiple filters
- Joins
- Reports
- Queries by many dimensions

---

# 23. Redis

Redis is an in-memory data store.

It supports more than simple values:

- Strings
- Hashes
- Lists
- Sets
- Sorted sets
- Streams
- Pub/sub
- Counters
- Expiring keys

## Good Redis Use Cases

- Application cache
- Sessions
- Rate limiting
- Counters
- Leaderboards
- Distributed locks
- Temporary data
- Real-time features
- Pub/sub
- Hot lookup data

## Strengths

- Very low latency
- Powerful data structures
- Atomic operations
- TTL support
- Good for hot data

## Trade-Offs

- RAM is expensive.
- Memory capacity is limited.
- Poor eviction settings can remove important data.
- Persistence may reduce performance.
- Redis is usually not the right place for large volumes of cold data.

Redis can be a database, but it is commonly used as a speed layer.

---

# 24. DynamoDB

DynamoDB is a durable, managed key-value and document database from AWS.

Good use cases:

- Large shopping carts
- Device state
- User preferences
- Massive key-based lookups
- Serverless applications
- Predictable high-scale workloads
- Globally replicated tables

Strengths:

- Managed infrastructure
- Horizontal scalability
- Durable storage
- Predictable key-based performance
- Global Tables support

Trade-offs:

- Access patterns must be planned.
- Scans are expensive.
- Poor partition-key selection creates hot partitions.
- Cost depends heavily on read/write patterns.
- Flexible ad hoc queries are limited.

---

# 25. Redis vs DynamoDB

| Area | Redis | DynamoDB |
|---|---|---|
| Primary role | Speed layer | Durable scale layer |
| Storage | Mainly memory | Durable managed storage |
| Latency | Extremely low | Low network-based latency |
| Best for | Cache, sessions, counters | Durable key-value workloads |
| TTL | Supported | Supported |
| Query planning | Simpler key access | Must be designed upfront |
| Cost concern | RAM | Request and storage patterns |
| Cold storage | Poor fit | Better fit |

Simple rule:

> Redis is commonly the speed layer. DynamoDB is commonly the durable key-value layer.

