# 42. Practical Database Selection Process

Use the following sequence:

1. List all entities.
2. List the main read queries.
3. List the main write operations.
4. Identify the read-to-write ratio.
5. Mark operations requiring ACID.
6. Mark data that can be eventually consistent.
7. Identify transaction boundaries.
8. Determine relationship depth.
9. Identify nested or hierarchical data.
10. Determine whether access is mainly by key.
11. Identify time-series or append-heavy data.
12. Determine ad hoc reporting requirements.
13. Estimate data volume and growth.
14. Estimate latency requirements.
15. Identify global distribution requirements.
16. Select the simplest suitable primary database.
17. Identify genuinely necessary supporting databases.
18. Add caching if required.
19. Plan backup and recovery.
20. Review operational complexity.
21. Review migration risk.
22. Validate the decision using actual access patterns.

