# 45. Important Trade-Offs

## SQL vs NoSQL

~~~text
SQL:
Correctness, joins and query flexibility

NoSQL:
Scale, availability and specialized access patterns
~~~

## ACID vs BASE

~~~text
ACID:
Stronger immediate correctness

BASE:
Higher availability with temporary inconsistency
~~~

## Strong vs Eventual Consistency

~~~text
Strong consistency:
Latest correct value, potentially higher latency

Eventual consistency:
Better availability and scale, but temporary stale data
~~~

## Document Embedding vs Referencing

~~~text
Embed:
Better read locality, but possible growth and duplication

Reference:
Better sharing and independent updates, but additional queries
~~~

## Redis vs DynamoDB

~~~text
Redis:
In-memory speed layer

DynamoDB:
Durable high-scale key-value storage
~~~

## Cassandra vs PostgreSQL

~~~text
Cassandra:
Massive distributed writes and known query patterns

PostgreSQL:
Transactions, joins and flexible queries
~~~

## Graph Database vs SQL Joins

~~~text
Graph:
Deep multi-hop relationship traversal

SQL:
Structured relationships, reporting and transactional integrity
~~~

## One Database vs Polyglot Persistence

~~~text
One database:
Simpler operation and consistency

Polyglot persistence:
Better workload fit, but higher complexity
~~~

## Flexible Schema vs Schema Enforcement

~~~text
Flexible schema:
Faster evolution, but more application discipline

Schema enforcement:
Stronger data quality, but controlled migrations are required
~~~

## Query Flexibility vs Write Scalability

~~~text
Flexible queries:
More indexes and general-purpose structures

Write scalability:
Fewer indexes and access-pattern-specific models
~~~

